              'First, the coil\'s magnetic moment:\n\n$ m = NIA = NI(\\pi r^{2}) = (16)(0.75)\\pi(0.10)^{2} = (12)\\pi(0.010) = 0.377\\ \\text{A·m}^{2} $\n\nA coil displaced slightly from alignment with the field oscillates exactly like a bar magnet, with period\n\n$ T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mathcal I}{mB}} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathcal I = \\frac{mBT^{2}}{4\\pi^{2}} $\n\nThe frequency is given directly, so $ T = 1/f = 1/2.0 = 0.50 $ s.\n\n$ \\mathcal I = \\frac{(0.377)(0.050)(0.50)^{2}}{4\\pi^{2}} = \\frac{(0.377)(0.050)(0.25)}{39.48} = \\frac{4.71\\times10^{-3}}{39.48} $\n\n$ \\mathcal I \\approx 1.19\\times10^{-4} \\approx 1.2\\times10^{-4}\\ \\text{kg·m}^{2} $\n\n**Sanity check.** This is a light, small coil, so a moment of inertia in the $ 10^{-4}\\ \\text{kg·m}^{2} $ range — comparable to a light plastic ring a few centimetres across — is entirely reasonable.',
