              '$ B_2 \\approx 4.4\\times10^{-3}\\ \\text{T} $',
              'At equilibrium the net torque on the dipole is zero: the torque pulling it toward $ B_1 $ (trying to shrink the $ 15^\\circ $ angle) must exactly balance the torque pulling it toward $ B_2 $ (trying to shrink the remaining $ 60^\\circ - 15^\\circ = 45^\\circ $ angle) — the two fields pull the dipole in opposite rotational senses:\n\n$ mB_1\\sin15^\\circ = mB_2\\sin45^\\circ $\n\nThe dipole moment $ m $ cancels — the equilibrium angle never depended on it:\n\n$ B_2 = B_1\\frac{\\sin15^\\circ}{\\sin45^\\circ} = (1.2\\times10^{-2})\\frac{0.259}{0.707} = (1.2\\times10^{-2})(0.366) $\n\n$ B_2 \\approx 4.39\\times10^{-3}\\ \\text{T} $\n\n**Sanity check on the geometry.** $ B_2 < B_1 $, and the dipole settles closer to $ B_1 $\'s direction ($ 15^\\circ $ away) than to $ B_2 $\'s ($ 45^\\circ $ away) — a dipole caught between two fields always leans closer to the stronger one, exactly as found here.',
